3. ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Repeated Dynamic Appends (Brute Force Simulation)

* Where it comes from:
* Beginner baseline commonly seen in initial attempts on LeetCode community discussions.
* Idea:
* Start with an empty vector and push all elements of nums into it one by one, then do the exact same loop a second time to push all elements again.
* C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getConcatenation(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> ans;
        for (int x : nums) {
            ans.push_back(x);
        }
        for (int x : nums) {
            ans.push_back(x);
        }
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity:
* Time complexity: O(n) amortized time, but repeated push_back calls cause multiple vector reallocations and buffer copies.
* Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space beyond the returned vector.
* What makes the next approach better:
* Reallocation overhead can be avoided completely by either pre-sizing or using modulo indexing.

Approach 2: Modulo Indexing (Two Passes in One Loop)

* Where it comes from:
* LeetCode editorial and circular array patterns.
* Idea:
* Create a result array of size 2n. Run a single loop from 0 to 2n - 1, and map the index back into nums using i % n.
* C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getConcatenation(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> ans(2 * n);
        for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
            ans[i] = nums[i % n];
        }
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity:
* Time complexity: O(n) because the loop runs 2n times.
* Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space.
* What makes the next approach better:
* The modulo operation (%) is slower than direct addition, and running a loop 2n times does twice as many loop checks as doing both halves in n steps.

Approach 3: Simultaneous Dual Assignment (Your Approach - Optimal Manual Simulation)

* Where it comes from:
* Official LeetCode editorial Approach 1 and top-voted community solutions.
* Idea:
* Pre-size the result vector to 2n. Loop from 0 to n - 1 and set both ans[i] and ans[i + n] at the same time.
* C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getConcatenation(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> ans(2 * n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            ans[i] = nums[i];
            ans[i + n] = nums[i];
        }
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity:
* Time complexity: O(n) because the loop runs exactly n times.
* Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space.
* What makes the next approach better:
* While this approach is optimal in time complexity, standard C++ provides memory-block copy methods (like std::vector::insert or memcpy) that utilize low-level system memory instructions for bulk copying.

Approach 4: STL Range Insert / Append (Idiomatic Modern C++)

* Where it comes from:
* Standard C++ STL best practices and top LeetCode C++ solutions.
* Idea:
* Initialize the return vector with a copy of nums, reserve space for 2n, and insert nums at the end of the new vector using range insert.
* C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getConcatenation(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> ans = nums;
        ans.insert(ans.end(), nums.begin(), nums.end());
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity:
* Time complexity: O(n) because bulk vector copy and range insert run in linear time and often leverage memmove / memcpy under the hood.
* Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space.

4. WHERE I STAND

* Which approach did I use?
* You used Approach 3 (Simultaneous Dual Assignment). It is optimal in terms of both asymptotic time and space.
* Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?
* Interviewers expect Approach 3 or Approach 4. Approach 3 demonstrates you understand index math and pre-allocation, while Approach 4 demonstrates proficiency with C++ STL container operations.
* Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:
* Question 1: What if we need to concatenate the array k times instead of just 2 times?
* Answer: Pre-allocate an array of size k * n. Use nested loops where the outer loop runs from 0 to k - 1 and the inner loop copies nums into ans[offset + i], or use a single loop from 0 to k * n - 1 with ans[i] = nums[i % n].
* Question 2: Why is vector ans(2 * n) better than starting with an empty vector and calling push_back?
* Answer: An empty vector dynamically doubles its internal capacity when full, triggering multiple memory allocations and copying old elements to new memory locations. Pre-sizing allocates the full block of contiguous memory once.
* Question 3: How can we achieve true O(1) auxiliary space if we are allowed to modify the input parameter?
* Answer: We can reserve 2 * n capacity on nums and insert its original elements at the end, returning nums directly.

5. WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

